## 1. Introduction
Hydrogels [1–3], which are composed of hydrophilic homopolymers or copolymers, are regarded as promising sorbents in the field of separation engineering because it can introduce various chelating groups into the polymer skeleton. Nowadays, kinds of monomers containing amino, acylamino, carboxyl, and sulfonic have been used to synthesize copolymerized hydrogels, which exhibit specific affinity toward toxic metal ions and molecules [4–6]. Among them, porous copolymeric hydrogels have multi-pleated structure, stable pore volume, larger specific surface area, and abundant binding sites, which provide the potential for efficient adsorption [7–9]. However, most of porous copolymeric hydrogels have weak mechanical properties and poor selectivity. Therefore, it is of great importance to develop hydrogel sorbents with high mechanical properties and stable shape in practical applications. Many efforts have been made to improve the mechanical strength of hydrogels in the decades, and various hydrogels fabricated by different methods have been reported, such as double-network hydrogels, macromolecular microsphere composite hydrogels and nanocomposite hydrogels. Oil-in-Water (O/W) high internal phase emulsions (HIPEs) are typical biphasic reactor platform for the preparation of macroporous polymers with extraordinary advantage in the inherent high porosity due to the high dispersed phase fraction and controllable pore size [10]. However, most of the hydrogel materials in the HIPEs reactor have weak mechanical properties, and polymer networks are easy to disintegrate or collapse, which limit the performance and applications of these materials. Recent research found that dynamic coexistence of heterostructures is crucial to the synergistic function of hydrogel materials. However, hydrogel sorbents prepared via HIPEs reactors demonstrating synergistic effects between heterophases have not been reported.
Molecular imprinting is a powerful synthetic method serving the purpose to develop molecularly imprinted polymers (MIPs) with specific receptors toward template [19,20]. In the process of molecular imprinting, ligand monomers with affinity functional groups, such as carboxy or amino groups, are arranged around the template molecules via covalent or noncovalent interactions. The highly specific binding sites are formed by using molecular template to control the distribution of functional monomers during the cross-linking polymerization [21]. In the recent years, many significant works about MIPs for separation and purification of flavones have been proved to have obvious advantages, such as more accessible sites, fast mass transfer and specific selectivity [22]. For instance, Zhang [23] et al. synthesized imprinted porous materials by the sol–gel method for selective separation of LTL. Recently, Pan [24] et al. fabricated a halloysite nanotubes-supported boronate affinity imprinted composites for highly specific LTL binding under neutral condition. However, boronate affinity imprinted hydrogel sorbents for the adsorption and separation of LTL are still limited.
Pickering HIPEs, using colloidal particles as stabilizers, can be adopted as a specific medium or template to produce particle reinforced macroporous hydrogel sorbents [25–28]. In addition, solid emulsifiers can also acted as physical cross-linkers to maintain the hydrogel networks [29–31]. UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ , the prototype zirconium $(\boldsymbol{Z}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{r}})$ -based metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are one of the most widely used materials because of their molecular sieving properties [32]. In addition, the strong coordination bonds between zirconium and carboxylate oxygen offer UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ a chemical, thermal, and mechanical stabilities in water (even under acidic or some alkaline conditions) [33]. Therefore, the UiO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ can act as stabilizer particles to stabilize the emulsion droplets. Herein, a hydrophilic boronate affinity imprinted hydrogel sorbent were designed by in situ polymerization of $\mathsf{U I O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ stabilized Pickering O/W HIPEs and packed reactive micro-inclusions (H-UIO$66\substack{-\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ -IHIPEs) was designed, and the resultant hydrogels can realize hydrophilic surface, high strength, porous structure and affinity sites simultaneously. In addition, rebinding equilibrium, kinetics, selectivity, and regeneration of $\mathrm{H-UIO-}66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -IHIPEs were investigated by batch mode experiments, respectively, and recognized LTL from real extract samples under aqueous phase molecular recognition was also demonstrated. Moreover, this work not only presents a new clue to fabricate hydrophilic porous imprinted hydrogels by in-situ emulsion interfaced polymerization of an O/W HIPEs system, but also provides a novel platform for LTL extraction.
## 2. Methods and materials
## 2.1. Chemicals
Ethyleneglycol dimethacrylate (EGDMA, $98\%$ ), N,N-dimethyl acrylamide (DMA, $99\%$ ), $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -diethoxyacetophenone (DEOP, $>95\%$ ), stearyl methacrylate (SMA, $96\%$ $250~\mathrm{ppm}.$ , glycidyl methacrylate (GMA, $97\%$ ), 2-aminoterephthalic acid $\mathrm{\ddot{H}}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ , ${>}98\%$ ), $3-$ aminobenzeneboronic acid (APBA, $\geq98\%$ , acryloyl chloride $(96\%)$ , hydrochloric acid (HCl, $36.0\sim38.0\%$ , sodium hydroxide (NaOH, $\geq96\%$ ), acetic acid $(99.8\%)$ , zircomiun tetrachloride $\mathrm{(}Z\mathrm{r}{\mathrm{Cl}_{4}},$ $299.9\%$ , N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF, $99.9\%$ ), ethanolamine $(99.7\%)$ , catechol (CC, $99\%$ , $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $(99\%)$ , hydroquinone (HDQ, $\geq99.5\%$ , $\mathfrak{p}$ -nitrophenol (P-NP, $98\%$ ), were obtained from aladdin Reagent Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). Luteolin (LTL, $298\%$ ) and $85\%$ crude LTL were provided by Kan Sheng Co., Ltd (Shangxi, China).
