## 2.3. Synthesis of $U I O–66–N H_{2}$ and MOF-5
UIO-66- ${\cdot}\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ was synthesized according to the procedure in ref. [34]. Briefly, $_{0.233\mathrm{~g~}}$ of $\mathrm{\Delta\zr{Cl_{4}}}$ and $_{0.181\mathrm{~g~}}$ of $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ were dissolved in $102.1\mathrm{\mL}$ of DMF solution. Then $22.9{\mathrm{~mL}}$ of acetic acid was added to the above solution and ultrasound for $20~\mathrm{{min}}$ . The mixed solution was transferred to a $200~\mathrm{{mL}}$ Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave, and kept at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ Then the mixture was cooled to room temperature, the product was washed with DMF for three times, and dried under vacuum at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ overnight.
Moreover, metal organic framework-5 (MOF-5) was also synthesized as a control [35], and the main steps were illustrated as follows: $\ensuremath{1.664\mathrm{~g~}}$ of $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and $\mathbf{0.352\g}$ of ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{BDC}}$ were added to $80~\mathrm{\mL}$ of DMF, and then the solution was kept at $130~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $4.0\mathrm{~h~}$ . Subsequently, the collected product was then heated at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ for $16\mathrm{~h~}$ in a vacuum oven.
## 2.4. Synthesis of hydrophilic acrylamide phenylboric acid monomer (H-BA)
The synthesis procedure of H-BA was followed ref. [36]. Typically, $8.22~\mathrm{g}$ of APBA $\left\langle60\mathrm{mmol}\right\rangle$ was dissolved in $120~\mathrm{mL}$ of NaOH solution $(240~\mathrm{\mmol})\cdot$ ), and then $9.6~\mathrm{\mL}$ of acryloyl chloride $\mathrm{120~mmol}_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ was added slowly with an ice water bath. After vigorously stirring for 30 min, HCl solution $(2.0\:\mathrm{M})$ was adjusted pH to 1.0. The resulting white precipitate was collected by filtration and washed with cold deionized water. The crude product was purified by recrystallization, gray needle-like crystal (i.e. H-BA) was then collected and dried overnight in a vacuum oven.
2.5. Preparation of porous boronate affinity imprinted hydrogel sorbents (UIO-66- ${\cdot}N H_{2}$ -IHIPEs) and hydrophilic boronate affinity imprinted hydrogel sorbent (H-UIO-66- ${\cdot}N H_{2}$ -IHIPEs)
UIO-66- ${\cdot}\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -IHIPEs were synthesized via in situ polymerization of Pickering HIPEs and packed reactive micro-inclusions. Briefly, $20\mathrm{mg}$ of LTL and $50\mathrm{mg}$ of H-BA were dispersed in $2.0\mathrm{mL}$ of water, and this solution was placed for $3.0\mathrm{h}$ . And then $60~\mathrm{mg}$ of UIO-66- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ , $_{0.5\mathrm{~g~}}$ of DMA, and $1.0~\mathrm{mg}$ of DEOP were also added into the water phase. The mixed oil phase, containing $6.5\mathrm{~g~}$ of SMA, $0.0325\mathrm{~g~}$ of EGDMA, $0.8~\mathrm{\mL}$ of GMA, $0.1~\mathrm{\mL}$ of span 80 and $1.0\mathrm{mg}$ of DEOP, was transferred into the water phase, and the mixture was homogenized at a speed of $2000~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $3.0\ \mathrm{min}$ to get stable O/W Pickering HIPEs. The UIO- $66\substack{-\mathsf{N H}_{2}}$ -IHIPEs were prepared from this emulsion template by ultraviolet photo-initiated polymerization at $50^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $1.0\mathrm{h}$ . Finally, $500~\mathrm{mg}$ of ${\mathrm{UIO}}{-}66{\-}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}.$ - IHIPEs were dipped into $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanolamine, and gently stirred at room temperature for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ . After a ring-opening reaction, porous boronate affinity imprinted hydrogel sorbents with hydrophilic surface (i.e. H-UIO-66- ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ -IHIPEs) were prepared. Additionally, non-imprinted hydrogel sorbents (i.e. H-UIO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -NIHIPEs) were also synthesized, and the procedures were same as H-UIO$66\substack{-\Nu H_{2}}$ -IHIPEs, except that the absence of template molecule LTL.
## 2.6. Batch mode adsorption experiments
The experimental parameters (such as pH, temperature and incubation time) were evaluated by batch mode adsorption experiments under ambient condition. For this purpose, the adsorption tests were performed by using H-UIO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ -NIHIPEs and HUIO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -IHIPEs as sorbents, respectively. The typical process was described as follow: H-UIO-66- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -NIHIPEs or H-UIO-66- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -IHIPEs were added into centrifuge tube containing $25{\mathrm{~mg}}$ ${\mathsf{L}}^{-1}$ of LTL solution, respectively. The testing solutions were placed in water bath at $298\ \mathrm{K},$ and the sorbents were collected after the required time $360\mathrm{\min};$ . The residual LTL concentration was measured by UV–vis absorption spectra. Eventually, the adsorption amount $(Q_{\mathrm{e}},\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1},$ ) was calculated by Eq. (1) [37].
$$
\sf{Q}_{\mathrm{e}}=\frac{(\sf C_{0}-\sf C_{e})V}{W}
$$
where $W({\bf g})$ and $V(\mathrm{mL}^{-1})$ represent the weight of sorbent and the testing solution volume, respectively. $C_{0}$ $(\mathrm{mg~L^{-1}}.$ ) and $C_{\mathrm{e}}$ (mg ${\sf L}^{-1}$ ) are the initial and equilibrium concentration of LTL.
To analyze the $\mathsf{p H}$ effect, $\mathsf{p H}$ of testing solution was adjusted to 4.0, 5.0, 6.0, 7.0, 8.0, 9.0 using 0.05 M HCl or NaOH solution. After binding experiment, the adsorption amount was calculated. For binding kinetic experiments, the initial concentration of LTL solution was $25~\mathrm{mgL}^{-1}$ $\mathrm{\Phi(pH=8.0)}$ , and the adsorption time was ranged from $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ to $240\ \mathrm{min}$ . For isothermal adsorption experiments, $\mathsf{p H}$ of LTL testing solutions was kept at 8.0 (concentration from $10\mathrm{\mg\L}^{-1}$ to $30\mathrm{\mg\L^{-1}}.$ , and the adsorption time was $240~\mathrm{{min}}$ . For selective adsorption experiments, H-UIO-66- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -NIHIPEs or H-UIO-66- ${\cdot\}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -IHIPEs were added into respective solutions of PNP, $\mathsf{H D Q}$ and LTL $(25{\mathrm{~mg~L^{-1}}}_{,}$ ), and the whole process was implemented for $6.0\mathrm{~h~}$ . The thermal control of the processes above was maintained at $298\ \mathrm{K}.$
