## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Design and fabrication of H-UIO-66- ${\cdot}N H_{2}$ -IHIPEs
As typical biphasic systems, HIPEs are important platforms for fabricating porous hydrogels. However, the prepared polymer networks are easy to disintegrate or collapse, resulting unstable volume and shape [38]. Pickering HIPEs using nanoparticles as emulsifier and stabilizers could enhance the mechanical performance of the generated hydrogels thus improving their mass transfer [39]. Moreover, Liu et al. observed that the introduction of micro-inclusions into the hydrogel matrix via HIPEs reactor could produce excellent shape memory properties [25]. In this work, boronate affinity imprinted hydrogel sorbents (H-UIO-66- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -IHIPEs) with porous structure and hydrophilic surface were prepared via UIO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ stabilized Pickering HIPEs packing reactive micro-inclusion in the internal phase (Scheme 1). The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images of the $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ show cubicshaped crystals with a mean diameter of $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ (Fig. S1A-C). The commonly used boronic acid monomers are insoluble in the water, thereby limits their further applications in the preparation of hydrogels. Firstly, a hydrophilic boronic acid monomer (H-BA) was synthesized using APBA as the initial substrate, and the 1- HNMR spectra of H-BA was checked in Fig. S2. The introduction of strongly electron-withdrawing substituents to the phenyl ring was involved, resulting in an electron-poor boron atom of boronic acid [36]. UIO- ${\cdot\}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -66 acts as the primary stabilizer of $0/\mathsf{W}$ type Pickering emulsion in the presence of few Span 80. The stability of the Pickering emulsions from Span 80 (PAH), MOF- $.5\ +$ Span 80 (MOF-5-HIPEs), UIO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ $^+$ Span 80 (UIO-66- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -HIPEs) was evaluated at different time periods, e.g. 0 d, 1.0 d, 7.0 d and $14{\mathrm{~d~}}$ , and the corresponding optical micrographs are showed in Fig. 1, respectively. Few span $80~\mathrm{do}$ not solely gets a stable Pickering emulsion, and the obvious phase separation was observed in less than one day. Meanwhile, in the case of the incorporation of MOF-5 with few span 80, MOF-5-HIPEs showed better stability, but the demulsification still can be found at the seventh day. It is expected that the UIO-66- ${\cdot\}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -HIPEs have the best stability, and the cream-like emulsion is retained for 14 days, indicating the availability of UIO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ for reducing the interfacial energy and their high adhesion energy of attachment at the oil/water interface.
The micro-inclusions in the internal (oil) phase contains SMA, GMA, EGDMA, and DEOP, and the external (aqueous) phase is consisted of DMA, DEOP, H-BA and LTL. Optical picture of UIO-66- ${\cdot\}\mathrm{{NH}}_{2}$ - HIPEs after addition of imprinted system is shown in Fig. 2A, and the optical micrograph of emulsion droplets displays a greater number of droplets with a wide distribution sizes (Fig. 2B). UV polymerization is a promising approach to prepare hydrogels, which features many advantages of rapid curing and mild conditions. After pouring the emulsion was poured into the mould, ${\mathsf{U I O-}}66{\mathsf{-N H}}_{2}$ -IHIPEs were prepared from Pickering emulsion template via UV polymerization of the aqueous phase (Fig. 2C). In order to reduce the non-specific adsorption and improve the selectivity, hydrophilic porous hydrogel adsorbent H-UIO-66- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -IHIPEs were prepared by ring-opening reaction of ethanolamine with GMA epoxy group and introducing hydrophilic groups on the surface of UIO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -IHIPEs. What’s more, the microinclusions in the oil phase were introduced into the elastic hydrogel matrix by emulsion interfacial polymerization, which greatly enhanced the mechanical strength and maintained the stable pore structure and shape of UIO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ -IHIPEs. Illustration of the heterogeneous interfacial structure of the ${\mathrm{H-UIO}}{\mathrm{-}}66{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ -IHIPEs (a) and the structure of poly(H-BA) (i.e. PHBA), poly(DMA) (i.e. PDMA), poly (SMA) (i.e. PSMA), and poly(GMA) (i.e. PGMA) (b) are exhibited in Scheme 2. The images of original, stretching and squeezing HUIO-66- ${\cdot\}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -IHIPEs are exhibited in Fig. 2D-H, confirming the remarkable mechanical performance and excellent flexibility. In Addition, by using different moulds, H-UIO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -IHIPEs with different shape can be achieved (Fig. S3, supporting information).
## 3.2. Characterization of H-UIO-66- ${\cdot}N H_{2}$ -IHIPEs
The SEM images of UIO-66- ${\cdot}\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -IHIPEs are shown in Fig. S4A-C. It can be found that UIO- $66\substack{-\mathsf{N H}_{2}}$ -IHIPEs have lots of interconnected pores, and the cell diameter and pore throat size are about $25~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ and $5.0~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , respectively, which are the typical profile for macroporous hydrogel prepared via Pickering HIPEs. In order to improve the hydrophilcity of UIO-66- ${\cdot\}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -IHIPEs, the hydrophilic groups are introduced onto the surface of UIO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -IHIPEs through ring-opening reaction of ethanolamine with PGMA epoxy groups. The SEM images of H-UIO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -IHIPEs and H-UIO-66- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -NIHIPEs are shown in Fig. 3A-D and Fig. S5A-D, respectively. The results show that both H-UIO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -IHIPEs and H-UIO-66- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -NIHIPEs maintain a good interconnected porous structure, which is beneficial to improve the mass transfer and accelerate adsorption kinetics. The SEM elemental mapping of H-UIO-66- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -IHIPEs and H-UIO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ -NIHIPEs are shown in Fig. 4 and Fig. S6, respectively. It can be observed that the signal of Zr element is uniformly distributed on the surface of the imprinted polymer, indicating that ${\mathsf{U I O-}}66{\mathsf{-N H}}_{2}$ is successfully dispersed at the oil–water interface and anchored or embedded into the polymer network as a particle emulsifier. In addition, element B is also presented in H-UIO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ -IHIPEs and H-UIO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ -NIHIPEs, suggesting that H-BA in the water phase is involved in the polymerization process as a hydrophilic boronate affinity functional monomer. Moreover, Fig. 5 lists the EDS elemental analysis of H-UIO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -IHIPEs and H-UIO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -NIHIPEs, which have similar signal peaks. The peaks of C, N, B, and Zr were observed, which was consistent with the results of the SEM elemental mapping.
The XPS spectrum of ${\mathrm{H}}{\mathrm{-UIO}}{\mathrm{-}}66{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ -IHIPEs is exhibited in Fig. 6. From the wide-scan spectrum, H-UIO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ -IHIPEs have six peaks at binding energies of 182.3 eV, 189.2 eV, 283.6 eV, 399.5 eV, 440.2 eV and $530.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ , representing the Zr 3d, B 1 s, C $1s,\mathsf{N}1s,\mathsf{Z r}3\mathsf{p}$ , and O1s region, respectively, which are agreement with the results of element analysis [40,41]. To know about the evolution of ${\mathrm{H-UIO}}{\mathrm{-}}66{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ -IHIPEs from UIO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ -IHIPEs, their contact angle results are described in Fig. S7. After the modification with ethanol amine, ${\mathrm{H}}{\mathrm{-UIO}}{\mathrm{-}}66{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ -IHIPEs surface was more hydrophilic, owing to the conversation of epoxy group into hydroxyl groups, which is expected to effectively avoid nonspecific adsorption and improve the selective adsorption efficiency [42].
In order to evaluate the thermal stability and study the main component of as-prepared sorbent, the thermogravimetric (TGA) curves of UIO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and ${\mathrm{H-UIO-}}66{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ -IHIPEs are described in Fig. 7. As shown in Fig. 7, the curves of two samples are similar. Within $200~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , H-UIO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -IHIPEs have a slight weight loss, which may be due to the fact that their hydrophilic surfaces absorb a small amount of moisture from the air. ${\mathsf{U I O-}}66{\mathsf{-N H}}_{2}$ is stable at this initial stage, but H-UIO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ -IHIPEs are rapidly decomposed in the case of higher temperature $(\geq280^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}})$ . The main reason is that the main component of H-UIO-66- ${\cdot}\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -IHIPEs is cross-linked polymer network, which is easier to decompose than inorganic material (such as $\mathsf{U I O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2};$ under high temperature. Subsequently, as the temperature elevates to $370^{\circ}\mathsf C$ UIO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ begins to decompose, which is mainly attributed to the infinite crystalline lattices through self-assembly between metal clusters and organic linkers. After $420~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the residue content of H-UIO$66\substack{-\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ -IHIPEs is higher than that of UIO-66- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , which is possibly due the existence of polymer matrix.
## 3.3. Effect of pH on adsorption performance of H-UIO-66- ${\cdot}N H_{2}$ -IHIPEs
The initial pH of the solution has an important influence on the charge transfer at the solid–liquid interface and is one of the important factors affecting the adsorption process. Therefore, it is necessary to inspect systematically the effect of pH on the LTL adsorption systematically. The effects of $\mathsf{p H}$ values (i.e., $\mathsf{p H}=4.0$ , 5.0, 6.0, 7.0, 8.0 and 9.0) on the adsorption capacity of $\mathsf{H}$ -UIO-66- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -IHIPEs were investigated. As illustrated in Fig. 8A, the adsorption capacity of H-UIO-66- ${\cdot}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -IHIPEs is greatly associated with the solution pH, which increases in the range of 4.0–8.0. The maximum adsorption capacity of $28.24\:\mathrm{\mg}\:\mathrm{\g}^{-1}$ was achieved at $\mathsf{p H}=8.0$ , and then reduced at $\mathsf{p H}=9.0$ . It is well-known that the boronic acid groups could form a cyclic boronate ester with cisdiol-containing compounds in an alkaline environment. Typically, boronate esters are easy to dissociate in an alkaline environment. Thus, in range of 4.0 to 8.0, the few uptake amount at low solution pH makes it possible to elute the captured LTL with acid solution. However, at higher solution pH, cis-diols of LTL are too unstable to form a covalent bond. Notably, this pH-responsive uptake and release performance is consistent with the commonly used sorbents based on boronate affinity, demonstrating the presence of phenylboronic acid groups [43]. Furthermore, combined with the inherent recognition of molecular imprinting and reversible covalent affinity, H-UIO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ -IHIPEs display higher adsorption capacity than previously reported sorbents. The adsorption performance of other materials previously reported for LTL is compared with H-UIO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ -IHIPEs (Table 3). As shown in Table 3, H
UIO-66- ${\cdot\}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -IHIPEs exhibit a higher adsorption amounts than other sorbents [22,45,46]. Furthermore, nano-imprinted particles are difficult to achieve solid–liquid separation or filtration, which is time-consuming, hard handling and easy leakage. Thus, the HUIO-66- ${\cdot\}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -IHIPEs are promising sorbents towards LTL.
## 3.4. Static adsorption kinetics of H-UIO-66- ${\cdot}N H_{2}$ -IHIPEs
These findings well demonstrate the imprinting recognition, while the boronate affinity provides targeted enrichment of LTL.
## 3.5. Adsorption isotherm
The equilibrium LTL adsorption isotherms of ${\mathrm{H}}{\mathrm{-UIO}}{\mathrm{-}}66{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{\mathrm{-}}$ IHIPEs were conducted to define the adsorption behaviors and adsorption capacity at the room temperature as presented in Fig. 8C. Obviously, experimental equilibrium uptake amount of H-UIO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ -IHIPEs at different initial LTL concentrations $(10\mathrm{mgL}^{-1}$ to $30\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ ) were $4.8\:\mathrm{mg}\:\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $11.2\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $22.3\mathrm{mgg^{-1}}$ , $28.1\mathrm{\mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , and $36.4\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. The isothermal data are plotted via Langmuir and Freundlich isotherm models, and the related parameters are summarized in Table 2. The adsorption isotherm of H-UIO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ -IHIPEs is better fitted by the Langmuir model $\cdot R^{2}=0.977\}$ than Freundlich model $\langle R^{2}=0.953\rangle$ , indicating the existence of an evenly chemisorption monolayer, which is consistent with the kinetics data [49]. Meanwhile, the theoretical maximum LTL adsorption capacity $(Q_{\mathrm{m}})$ of ${\mathrm{H-UIO-}}66{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ -IHIPEs is $39.77\ \mathrm{mg\g^{-1}}$ at $25~^{\circ}C$ , showing better adsorption performance than H-UIO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ -NIHIPEs. The large adsorption capacity can be greatly attributed to the introduction of enough affinity groups on the surface of porous hydrogels, The $Q_{\mathrm{m}}$ of $\mathrm{H{-}U I O{-}}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ - NIHIPEs $(27.04~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ) may be attributed to the presence of boronic acid groups, and the boronate affinity action is their only recognition mechanism.
## 3.6. Selectivity of H-UIO-66- ${N H}_{2}$ -IHIPEs for LTL separation
To measure the specificity of H-UIO-66- ${\cdot}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -IHIPEs, the recognition abilities of LTL is compared with three structural analogs (i.e., QRT, P-NP, and CC) at the same batch conditions. In a single adsorbate solution, the adsorption amounts of ${\mathrm{H}}{\mathrm{-UIO}}{\mathrm{-}}66{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{\mathrm{-}}$ IHIPEs towards LTL and three structural analogs are depicted in Fig. 8D, which are $28.82~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $2.63~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $2.84~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , and $2.38~\mathrm{~mg~}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ for LTL, QRT, P-NP, and CC, respectively. Such antibody-like specificity is observed for ${\mathrm{H-UIO-}}66{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ -IHIPEs towards LTL. The molecular structure of QRT is extremely similar to the LTL, but its adsorption amount is much lower than LTL. Thus, it is beneficial to the diffusion of LTL to the binding sites, since its molecular structure is more consistent with the imprinted cavity shape of the H-UIO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ -IHIPEs. The molecular size of P-NP and CC is smaller than LTL, and cis-diol groups are not exposed. Therefore, the adsorption capacity of smaller molecular size compounds is still lower than target LTL onto ${\mathrm{H-UIO-}}66{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ -IHIPEs, and the memory action of affinity groups is beneficial to enhance the specific recognition of LTL.
## 3.7. Reusability performance and application of H-UIO-66- ${\cdot}N H_{2}$ -IHIPEs
To test the reutilization, H-UIO- $66\substack{-}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -IHIPEs were evaluated by five sequential cycles of adsorption-regeneration. pHresponsive adsorption and release is one of the important properties of H-UIO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -IHIPEs, so the mixture solution of methanol/ acetic acid $(\boldsymbol{\mathbf{v}}/\boldsymbol{\mathbf{v}},\ 8;2)$ is considered as the elution agent. The adsorption capacities of the ${\mathrm{H-UIO-}}66{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ -IHIPEs for LTL in the adsorption–desorption cycles are shown in Fig. S8. The adsorptive capacity reduces $6.24\%$ after five cycles, and this slight decrease is probably due to the mass loss of $\mathrm{H{-}U I O{-}}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -IHIPEs and partly due to nonrenewable imprinting sites. As a result, H-UIO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ - IHIPEs exhibits unexpected potential for selective separation LTL from practical samples. Considering the practicality of H-UIO-66- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -IHIPEs, the purification activity of $85\%$ crude LTL was investigated, and Mass Spectrometry (MS) analysis is used for identification and detection of the purity of the purified LTL. As shown in Fig. 9, the molecular ion at $m/z~287.15~[\mathrm{M-H}]^{-}$ is consistent with the LTL. Upon further comparison with this peak intensity, the purified product has a higher level of LTL (about $97.38\%$ than crude extract (about $85.0\%$ , which further demonstrated that HUIO-66- ${\cdot\}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -IHIPEs could be used for the separation and enrichment of LTL in real samples.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, a synergistic strategy of HIPEs droplets reactor packing reactive micro-inclusion were utilized to prepare hydrophilic boronate-based imprinted hydrogel $\mathrm{\left(H{-}U I O{-}66{-}N H_{2}{-}\right.}$ IHIPEs), and then as-prepared hydrogel sorbents were applied for highly selective adsorption of LTL. The welldispersed ethanol amine and solid emulsifier UIO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ could effectively improve the hydrophilicity and mechanical performance of H-UIO-66- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -IHIPEs, respectively. Combined with the interfacial cross-linking reaction of packed microinclusions, the stable pore volume and shape of H-UIO-66- ${\cdot\}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -IHIPEs were ensured. Hydrophilic acrylamide phenylboric acid monomer (HBA), as a functional monomer in the imprinting process, has a specific affinity for LTL responsiveness to pH. Therefore, the results of the present investigation extends on previous findings on boronate-based hybrid hydrogel [46] and boronate-based membrane composites [50] by demonstrating that the interfaceinduced co-assembly polymerization strategy is an efficient method to fabricate boronate affinity imprinted hydrogel sorbents. Through the batch mode experiments, the large adsorption capacity, rapid binding kinetics, and excellent regeneration and selectivity ability of ${\mathrm{H-UIO-}}66{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ -IHIPEs were confirmed, which agrees with the pseudo-second-order kinetic equation and Langmuir model. All the above results indicate that the combination of binary synergistic components and HIPEs reactor is an efficient approach for fabrication hydrophilic imprinted hydrogel, and the resultant sorbents have great potential in the specific molecular recognition and separation of natural products.
